// The CPU flags as left by the asm block just before the test (spec 7, 10.1). Each function has an
// empty body, so a call adds no instructions: `if cpu.carry()` is one bcc. The result is defined only
// directly after an asm block, as the statement `if f()`, `while f()` or `v = f()` (also with `!`);
// after compiled Tern code the flags are unspecified.
//
//     asm { jsr read_byte }       // carry set at end of file
//     if cpu.carry() { done = true }

// The C flag.
inline asm fn carry() -> bool using carry {}

// The Z flag: true when the last result was 0.
inline asm fn zero() -> bool using zero {}

// The N flag: bit 7 of the last result.
inline asm fn negative() -> bool using negative {}

// The V flag: a signed overflow of adc or sbc, or bit 6 of the byte that bit tested.
inline asm fn overflow() -> bool using overflow {}
